  "summary": "Distinctive temple architecture from the Hoysala Empire featuring star-shaped platforms and intricate carvings.",
  "description": "Characterized by stellate (star-shaped) temple platforms with ornate sculptural friezes depicting mythological narratives. Developed under the Hoysala dynasty, reflecting a blend of Nagara and Dravida elements with unique regional craftsmanship.",
  "culturalContext": "The Hoysala style (1026-1343) — Vesara hybrid of Dravida and Nagara — built Karnataka's most intricate temples: Belur's Chennakesava, Halebidu's Hoysaleswara, Somnathpura's Keshava — on star-shaped (16-24 point) platforms, with chloritic schist so fine it carves like wood: friezes of elephants, horses and myth literally wrapping bases, lathe-turned pillars, and scalloped towers. soapstone allowed hundreds of deities in single registers; UNESCO-listed in 2023, the style is medieval Karnataka's signature.",
